@flexiarg apparatus/success-must-not-resemble-failure
@title Success Must Not Resemble Failure
@keywords normal-path, fault-shape, cancellation, ledger-vocabulary, delivery
@audience apparatus builders, protocol designers, futon developers
@tone technical
@style pattern
@status proposed (extracted from the APM failure record 2026-09-08; claude-9 draft, claude-1 redline; pending Joe's ruling)
@references [apparatus/evidence-to-disposition-once]
@see-also apparatus/every-wait-has-a-deadline
@why futon3c/holes/T-typed-submission-wrapper-cancellation-evidence.md (987e8574): the wrapper cancels a job once an authenticated submission exists, so a DELIVERED role turn normally ends `cancelled` with a nil session id; 13 of 126 campaign parks descend from that one shape. Agency twin: `accepted` precedes and does not imply `running` — trusting it cost parked-forever seats.
@how own mechanism: give the commonest good outcome a first-class success state in the primary record, so no consumer needs inference to tell delivery from fault.
@violation-signature Consumers need cleverness to distinguish the happy path from a fault; error vocabulary (`cancelled`, nil ids, timeouts) appears in the record of a normal successful operation.
! conclusion: The normal path may not produce fault-shaped evidence; the commonest good outcome gets a first-class success state in the primary record.

  + context: A wrapper, relay, or driver sits between the worker and the ledger, and its bookkeeping actions (cancelling a superseded job, rotating a session) are recorded in the same vocabulary as failures.

  + IF:
    The primary record of an operation is written by machinery whose own lifecycle events are visible in it.

  + HOWEVER:
    The cheap implementation reuses an existing terminal state (`cancelled`, `timeout`) for a bookkeeping event on the success path. Every downstream consumer must then reconstruct "this cancellation is actually a delivery" — and each reconstructs it differently.

  + THEN:
    Extend the ledger's vocabulary instead: a distinct terminal state for the success case (e.g. `delivered-by-submission`), written at the moment the success evidence exists. Bookkeeping events that follow success must not overwrite or shadow it.

  + BECAUSE:
    When success looks like failure, every consumer of the record must be smart, and the divergence between their reconstructions becomes an open-ended source of attribution bugs — the inferential burden lands on N readers instead of one writer.

    + evidence: APM jit-all-open-v3: 13/126 parks were `:student-terminal-session-id-erased` — successful deliveries whose wrapper cancellation destroyed the session evidence; most of the fault-attribution machinery the campaign accreted exists to see through this one lie.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    - List the terminal states your primary ledger can record; for each, ask whether a successful operation can end in it. Each yes is this defect.
    - Where a status precedes the fact it implies (accepted vs running), require consumers to wait for an event that entails the fact.
